  "id": "e00045",
  "title": "Whole Number ÷ Unit Fraction Word Problems",
  "intro": "Use fraction strips to show how many small pieces fit into a whole-number length.",
  "html": "<h2>Teaching notes for parents and teachers</h2><h3>How to teach it</h3><p>Mark the given unit-fraction length along the full cable. Count the pieces and check their combined length.</p><h3>Example to work through together</h3><p>Use the model below as you explain the example. Have your student point to the quantities as you discuss them.</p><div class=\"model unit-fraction-pieces-model\"><p class=\"sample-equation\">3 ÷ 1/2 = 6 pieces</p><svg viewBox=\"0 0 380 190\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Three one-foot lengths. Each foot is split into two half-foot pieces, making six equal pieces altogether.\" style=\"max-width:480px;width:100%;height:auto\"><text x=\"190\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"currentColor\">Total cable length: 3 feet</text><rect class=\"half-foot-piece\" x=\"25\" y=\"55\" width=\"55\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#217668\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><text x=\"52.5\" y=\"119\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\">1</text><rect class=\"half-foot-piece\" x=\"80\" y=\"55\" width=\"55\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#e7f3ef\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><text x=\"107.5\" y=\"119\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\">2</text><rect class=\"half-foot-piece\" x=\"135\" y=\"55\" width=\"55\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#217668\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><text x=\"162.5\" y=\"119\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\">3</text><rect class=\"half-foot-piece\" x=\"190\" y=\"55\" width=\"55\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#e7f3ef\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><text x=\"217.5\" y=\"119\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\">4</text><rect class=\"half-foot-piece\" x=\"245\" y=\"55\" width=\"55\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#217668\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><text x=\"272.5\" y=\"119\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\">5</text><rect class=\"half-foot-piece\" x=\"300\" y=\"55\" width=\"55\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#e7f3ef\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><text x=\"327.5\" y=\"119\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\">6</text><path d=\"M25 44V36H135V44\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"152\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\">1 foot</text><path d=\"M135 44V36H245V44\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><text x=\"190\" y=\"152\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\">1 foot</text><path d=\"M245 44V36H355V44\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#154d56\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"152\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\">1 foot</text><text x=\"190\" y=\"183\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\">Each numbered piece is 1/2 foot long.</text></svg><p>Count the pieces, not just the three whole feet. Each foot contains two half-foot pieces, so three feet contains three groups of two: six pieces.</p></div><h3>Ask your student</h3><blockquote><p>How many of these pieces fit in one foot?</p></blockquote><h3>If your student needs help</h3><p>If the student expects division to make the answer smaller, compare the number of pieces with the length being measured.</p>",
  "source": "content/en-US/word-problems-whole-divided-unit-fraction.json",
  "origin": "reused"
}
